1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Roll up your sleeves.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Get down. Get dirty.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
As these mechanical monsters drill, plow, and haul mountains and soil.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
From hundreds of feet below the Earth, to the deepest depths of the oceans,

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
to neighboring planets within our solar system.

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, more Earthmovers on Modern Marvelous.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
They make the ground tremble.

8
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:40,000
They change the landscape.

9
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:44,000
They move the earth.

10
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:48,000
They may seem like the world's biggest toys,

11
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:51,000
but this is no game.

12
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:54,000
These are multi-million dollar workhorses

13
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:57,000
that support billion dollar industries.

14
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:00,760
that support billion-dollar industries,

15
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:06,540
scooping out Earth's riches, relocating Earth's muck,

16
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:12,240
crushing big rocks into little rocks,

17
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:15,460
sometimes saving the environment, sometimes

18
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:18,460
creating environmental havoc.

19
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:21,460
The earthmoving industry is constantly pushing the boundaries

20
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,060
of size and power.

21
00:01:24,061 --> 00:01:27,900
Today's Earth movers dig deeper, tunnel farther,

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,640
and transport more Earth than their predecessors did

23
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:32,940
just a decade ago.

24
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:35,780
Yet ironically, the world's ultimate Earth mover

25
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:38,740
is as old as the Earth itself.

26
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,840
It's the raw power of Mother Nature.

27
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:43,520
There's a tremendous amount of power

28
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:46,920
that can be unleashed by storms or stream movement.

29
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:49,760
It far exceeds our ability, what we can do as humans,

30
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:54,060
to move soil in a rapid period of time.

31
00:01:54,061 --> 00:01:58,660
On the Gulf Coast of Mississippi, what took men and machines decades to build

32
00:01:58,661 --> 00:02:04,360
was destroyed in a matter of hours by one of the most destructive hurricanes on record.

33
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:10,600
Hurricane Katrina made landfall in Mississippi on 29 August 2005,

34
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:15,180
and it was a Category 4 hurricane, and the devastation was complete.

35
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:20,940
Winds clocked at 140 miles per hour, toppled trees, and leveled homes.

36
00:02:20,941 --> 00:02:26,180
In the aftermath of the deluge, the federal government declared 48 of

37
00:02:26,181 --> 00:02:33,360
Mississippi's 83 counties disaster areas. In ravaged neighborhoods like this one

38
00:02:33,361 --> 00:02:38,980
in Hancock County, only a few homes were left standing.

39
00:02:38,981 --> 00:02:44,620
When Hurricane Katrina hit, there was 25 to 30 foot of water that came over this

40
00:02:44,621 --> 00:02:48,720
spot that we're standing right now that caused this entire devastation that's

41
00:02:48,721 --> 00:02:52,760
around us. Earth movers that helped sculpt this

42
00:02:52,761 --> 00:02:55,760
neighborhood decades ago were given the task of removing the

43
00:02:55,761 --> 00:03:02,160
debris that now littered it. Overseeing the disaster relief

44
00:03:02,161 --> 00:03:05,760
effort is a fusion of government agencies, spearheaded by the

45
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:11,280
Federal Emergency Management Agency, or FEMA.

46
00:03:11,281 --> 00:03:14,880
FEMA has turned to the Corps of Engineers for Debris Removal,

47
00:03:14,881 --> 00:03:20,160
And they have a massive project that they need to work on.

48
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:23,040
Debris removal is always one of the very first parts of recovery.

49
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:28,840
In support of FEMA, the Army Corps of Engineers initiated Task Force Hope.

50
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:36,200
One of its main objectives, the removal of 24 million cubic feet of debris

51
00:03:36,201 --> 00:03:39,700
in 17 different counties across the state of Mississippi.

52
00:03:39,701 --> 00:03:47,500
The end state of Task Force Hope is to assist the people of Mississippi to begin to rebuild

53
00:03:47,501 --> 00:03:51,220
their lives to a state it was before Hurricane Katrina hit.

54
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:54,440
And we will do that with the help of earthmoving equipment.

55
00:03:55,520 --> 00:04:01,840
Around the clock, earthmovers continued to bulldoze, lift, and dump the ruins left over

56
00:04:01,841 --> 00:04:02,900
from Katrina's assault.

57
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:09,360
Workers navigated their machines to the devastated neighborhoods through city streets.

58
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:14,740
This limited the size of the vehicles they could use, adding months to the cleanup effort.

59
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:18,280
It also created a potential life-threatening risk.

60
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:26,540
As the bucket loaders get the debris and swing over and deposit the debris in the dump trucks,

61
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:32,380
they have to be ever vigilant of the power lines that are in there so that they don't get electrocuted.

62
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:39,280
Earth movers placed the vestiges of homes, some a century old, into dump trucks.

63
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:44,700
From there, the remains would be shredded and placed into landfills.

64
00:04:44,701 --> 00:04:49,700
The typical equipment used are your track hose, which we have behind us.

65
00:04:49,701 --> 00:04:54,700
They are normally used to pull the debris apart, to break it into pieces small enough to handle,

66
00:04:54,701 --> 00:05:00,700
and that bobcat that we have here behind us, which will take it and load it on the trucks.

67
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:07,700
For students in the past Christiane, Mississippi School District,

68
00:05:07,701 --> 00:05:10,700
Every ton of debris these earthmovers hauled away

69
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:13,200
was a step toward normalcy.

70
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:15,900
Get your pencil ready.

71
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,240
The middle school was completely damaged during the hurricane,

72
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:21,840
and the high school was partially damaged.

73
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:25,260
Once we got the debris moved, that allowed us room

74
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:29,560
to bring in fill, bring the site up to a set elevation,

75
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:32,660
which then allowed us to bring these units in

76
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:36,060
and move forward to get the school open for the kids.

77
00:05:36,061 --> 00:05:39,240
After a month, the earthmovers of Task Force Hope

78
00:05:39,241 --> 00:05:41,720
were living up to the promise of the operation's name.

79
00:05:43,260 --> 00:05:46,100
But their arduous task had only begun.

80
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:52,540
From hurricanes to earthquakes, to natural erosion,

81
00:05:53,660 --> 00:05:56,540
nature has been moving Earth for billions of years.

82
00:05:57,020 --> 00:06:00,820
But man's earthmoving efforts date back roughly 400,000 years,

83
00:06:00,821 --> 00:06:04,120
when our ancient ancestor, Homo erectus,

84
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:06,000
started rolling big rocks around.

85
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:11,500
The cave dwellers needed to move soil around and rocks around because when

86
00:06:11,501 --> 00:06:15,600
you're living in a cave you've got to have areas where you can store things. If

87
00:06:15,601 --> 00:06:19,660
you had animals and you wanted to fence them in you're going to have to create a

88
00:06:19,661 --> 00:06:23,460
fencing system. Rocks were one of the first technologies we could do to create

89
00:06:23,461 --> 00:06:28,500
fences. Humans were soon digging holes, removing dirt in woven baskets, and

90
00:06:28,501 --> 00:06:35,580
digging out mud for bricks. We're still digging holes but we're better at it now.

91
00:06:35,581 --> 00:06:37,980
A lot better.

92
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:41,940
This is no woven basket.

93
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:47,020
It's a 320-ton Komatsu Hallpack 930E,

94
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:49,880
one of the largest mining trucks in the world.

95
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:52,940
Clearly, this isn't your typical pickup.

96
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:55,740
A man can walk underneath this vehicle

97
00:06:55,741 --> 00:06:56,960
without even bending over.

98
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:01,880
The six tires have a radius of over 12 and a half feet.

99
00:07:01,881 --> 00:07:08,060
and cost more than $30,000 each.

100
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,120
If you think you're spending a lot of the pump,

101
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:17,320
it takes 1,200 gallons of diesel to top off the tank of a 930E truck.

102
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:21,360
And if you're going to get behind the wheel of this mechanical beast,

103
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:23,480
you better not be afraid of heights.

104
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:25,800
The truck is 23 feet tall.

105
00:07:25,801 --> 00:07:33,980
It's basically just like driving a big old house with a cab sitting on top.

106
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:39,860
Komatsu Mining Systems is one of a handful of manufacturers of these large off-road trucks.

107
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:46,493
Its plant in Peoria, Illinois, began as the dream of an inventor named R.G.

108
00:07:46,494 --> 00:07:47,220
Letourneau.

109
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:54,060
He manufactures scrapers, made his own welders, made wire rope, and basically founded this.

110
00:07:54,061 --> 00:07:57,820
So from that, we grew from construction to off-highway trucks.

111
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:00,400
Letourneau kept his engineers on their toes.

112
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:05,840
He would go around the shop and some piece wouldn't work or wouldn't fit or whatever.

113
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:09,900
He would either redesign it on the shop floor with chalk,

114
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:13,420
or he'd have an envelope in his pocket and he'd draw it on the back of the envelope

115
00:08:13,421 --> 00:08:16,300
and he'd drive the engineers, and that's just trying to keep up with him,

116
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:19,500
because those were the changes for the next machine.

117
00:08:19,501 --> 00:08:27,060
The innovative design characteristics of Letourneau's first haul pack trucks are now standard on all mining trucks in the industry.

118
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:35,440
He'd make the truck short so it would have a short turning circle, make it maneuverable.

119
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:41,340
But the major thing he did was that the truck weighed less than it carried.

120
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:48,320
And all the trucks prior to the off-highway trucks prior to that weighed a lot more than the payload that they carried.

121
00:08:48,321 --> 00:08:50,080
There's a lot of efficiencies gained with that.

122
00:08:51,560 --> 00:08:54,800
Trucks evolved from the old dump wagons of the 19th century,

123
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:57,800
designed to haul and dump their load of earth.

124
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:02,200
The advent of the internal combustion engine

125
00:09:02,201 --> 00:09:05,880
compelled companies like Mack to improve on horse-drawn wagons.

126
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:11,240
Standard-sized trucks that worked on giant projects like the Hoover Dam

127
00:09:11,241 --> 00:09:14,780
often weighed more than their payloads.

128
00:09:14,781 --> 00:09:22,760
But when the true gigantic off-road trucks emerged in the late 1940s, their much larger

129
00:09:22,761 --> 00:09:29,140
payload capacity made Letourneau's lighter design much more practical.

130
00:09:29,141 --> 00:09:33,460
After Letourneau retired, his company passed through many hands and is currently owned

131
00:09:33,461 --> 00:09:39,020
by Komatsu, which now builds a vehicle with ten times the hauling capacity of his first

132
00:09:39,021 --> 00:09:42,660
haul pack.

133
00:09:42,661 --> 00:09:46,660
Things have changed since the days of blueprints on the back of envelopes.

134
00:09:46,661 --> 00:09:53,820
I supervise six other engineers and we're responsible for the design of the hydraulic

135
00:09:53,821 --> 00:10:00,620
systems on the trucks as well as the brake systems, brake application systems and final

136
00:10:00,621 --> 00:10:02,620
drive design.

137
00:10:02,621 --> 00:10:08,060
Komatsu's largest trucks are partly powered by diesel and partly by electricity generated

138
00:10:08,061 --> 00:10:10,260
by the vehicle itself.

139
00:10:10,261 --> 00:10:16,060
The diesel engine is connected to electrical generators.

140
00:10:16,061 --> 00:10:21,400
Energy that's produced when a truck descends a hill is stored in batteries on the vehicle.

141
00:10:21,401 --> 00:10:25,720
That stored energy is then used to propel the trucks when they ascend hills, sending

142
00:10:25,721 --> 00:10:29,500
power to the large wheels.

143
00:10:29,501 --> 00:10:31,500
The trucks have no transmission.

144
00:10:31,501 --> 00:10:37,480
Yes, it is more elaborate than your typical Ford or Chevy pickup.

145
00:10:37,481 --> 00:10:39,820
The Komatsu plant, where these earth-moving brutes are

146
00:10:39,821 --> 00:10:43,860
manufactured, looks like a boneyard for dinosaurs.

147
00:10:43,861 --> 00:10:48,300
The assembly process for 930 takes approximately 10 days

148
00:10:48,301 --> 00:10:51,100
from the time the frame is set in this building

149
00:10:51,101 --> 00:10:53,600
and the assembly process starts.

150
00:10:53,601 --> 00:10:55,660
Steel that makes up the 930's body

151
00:10:55,661 --> 00:10:59,000
can be 1 and 1 1⁄2 inches thick.

152
00:10:59,001 --> 00:11:03,280
Its engine produces 2,700 horsepower.

153
00:11:03,281 --> 00:11:08,280
The truck reaches a top speed of about 45 miles an hour.

154
00:11:08,281 --> 00:11:12,280
The vehicle is 27 feet wide and 50 feet long,

155
00:11:12,281 --> 00:11:17,280
and the volume of its bed is roughly 200 cubic yards.

156
00:11:17,281 --> 00:11:20,280
Once the truck is tested, it has to be disassembled

157
00:11:20,281 --> 00:11:23,280
and transported to the job site.

158
00:11:23,281 --> 00:11:28,280
It typically ships by rail, requiring three rail cars

159
00:11:28,281 --> 00:11:32,280
to ship the wheels, the truck itself, and its components,

160
00:11:32,281 --> 00:11:35,120
but that does not include the bed of the truck.

161
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:38,360
Once on site, workers use the trucks continuously.

162
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:44,060
Three shifts a day, 365 days a year for the life of the vehicle.

163
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:50,840
That could add up to some 15 years or around 100,000 hours of driving.

164
00:11:51,880 --> 00:11:55,140
A truck the size of the 930E costs over $2 million,

165
00:11:55,780 --> 00:11:58,840
so mining outfits must drive them hard to show a profit.

166
00:11:58,841 --> 00:12:03,620
The name of the game in producing efficiently is asset utilization.

167
00:12:03,621 --> 00:12:08,020
When a customer, such as a large mine, buys a fleet of equipment,

168
00:12:08,021 --> 00:12:10,520
they're making a substantial capital investment,

169
00:12:10,521 --> 00:12:15,360
and they have to get every ounce of production out of that investment that they can get.

170
00:12:15,361 --> 00:12:20,320
These trucks are used in all kinds of mines, from coal to gold.

171
00:12:20,321 --> 00:12:23,800
But they're most commonly used in open-pit copper mines.

172
00:12:23,801 --> 00:12:27,840
Operating eight or ten giant haul packs is cheaper and more efficient

173
00:12:27,841 --> 00:12:30,360
than using 40 or 50 smaller vehicles.

174
00:12:31,780 --> 00:12:34,540
Having large equipment is a real advantage

175
00:12:34,541 --> 00:12:37,220
because you have one operator moving that soil,

176
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:40,020
whereas if you had a whole slew of people out there,

177
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:42,060
you're paying wages on these people.

178
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:45,620
And in an industry with a poor environmental reputation,

179
00:12:46,260 --> 00:12:49,340
these trucks produce less emissions per ton than smaller trucks

180
00:12:49,341 --> 00:12:50,960
and take fewer trips.

181
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:56,120
The trucks are sized to match the shovels

182
00:12:56,121 --> 00:12:58,720
that do the actual digging on site,

183
00:12:58,721 --> 00:13:01,620
with optimum truck size being four passes,

184
00:13:01,621 --> 00:13:03,620
or swings of the shovel per load.

185
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:07,320
Big trucks like these have to be accommodated

186
00:13:07,321 --> 00:13:09,060
with special wide roads.

187
00:13:09,880 --> 00:13:11,120
It costs a lot of money in a mine

188
00:13:11,121 --> 00:13:12,620
to build a road for the trucks.

189
00:13:12,621 --> 00:13:13,800
They're changing all the time

190
00:13:13,801 --> 00:13:16,620
because they're making the mine wider all the time.

191
00:13:16,621 --> 00:13:18,540
And if you're driving a regular sized vehicle

192
00:13:18,541 --> 00:13:21,240
near one of these behemoths, watch out,

193
00:13:21,241 --> 00:13:23,680
because up in the driver's seat of a 930,

194
00:13:23,681 --> 00:13:24,880
The blind spot is enormous.

195
00:13:26,480 --> 00:13:32,500
There are numerous incidents of pickup trucks being run over in mines by these trucks.

196
00:13:32,501 --> 00:13:38,720
And I will tell you, in a 320-ton truck, you can run over a pickup truck and not know it.

197
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:43,340
Getting more out of earth movers means getting more out of the earth.

198
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:51,740
For Komatsu and the other big truck manufacturers, that means designing bigger and bigger vehicles to get more ore out of the mine.

199
00:13:51,741 --> 00:14:00,040
For other earthmovers, it might not be precious metals they're looking for, but tons of soggy

200
00:14:00,041 --> 00:14:05,140
muck.

201
00:14:05,141 --> 00:14:08,240
The Letourneau Company, founded by earthmoving pioneer R.G.

202
00:14:08,241 --> 00:14:14,740
Letourneau, built nearly 70% of all the heavy earthmoving machines used by the Allied forces

203
00:14:14,741 --> 00:14:19,240
in World War II.

204
00:14:19,241 --> 00:14:22,400
Earth movers can transform the world right before our eyes.

205
00:14:23,940 --> 00:14:27,420
But some change a part of the world that most of us never see.

206
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:31,560
When it comes to reshaping the underwater landscape,

207
00:14:32,120 --> 00:14:34,460
the earth-moving workhorse is the dredge.

208
00:14:36,100 --> 00:14:38,660
The chief function of the world's dredging force

209
00:14:38,661 --> 00:14:41,360
is to clear debris from the bottom of shipping channels

210
00:14:41,361 --> 00:14:44,820
so the commercial vessels, heavily laden with goods,

211
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:46,140
don't run aground.

212
00:14:49,240 --> 00:14:52,820
In South Carolina, 40 feet above the earth it's moving,

213
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:56,720
the mechanical dredge Atlantic works to deepen Charleston Harbor.

214
00:14:58,840 --> 00:15:01,200
The Atlantic is a bucket dredge.

215
00:15:02,980 --> 00:15:06,560
This type of dredge is most effective at removing soft sediment.

216
00:15:07,720 --> 00:15:10,780
The Atlantic is one of the largest bucket dredges on the eastern seaboard.

217
00:15:11,700 --> 00:15:16,200
In one scoop, it's capable of shuttling 32 cubic yards,

218
00:15:16,201 --> 00:15:19,200
the equivalent volume of an average-sized bedroom.

219
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:23,100
The job in Charleston is two-pronged.

220
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:26,560
The Atlantic is scooping muck that is shoaled in

221
00:15:26,561 --> 00:15:29,460
or migrated from one part of the harbor into the channel.

222
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:34,000
It's also digging the channel deeper than before

223
00:15:34,001 --> 00:15:37,140
in order to accommodate ships loaded to deeper depths.

224
00:15:38,820 --> 00:15:41,640
We were actually doing about four feet of maintenance

225
00:15:41,641 --> 00:15:44,480
and another five or six feet of additional depth

226
00:15:44,481 --> 00:15:46,460
to increase the depth of the channel.

227
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:49,580
The Atlantic and most dredges today

228
00:15:49,581 --> 00:15:52,920
are sophisticated multi-million dollar precision instruments

229
00:15:52,921 --> 00:15:57,400
using the latest global positioning systems and onboard computers.

230
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:00,580
But it wasn't always like this.

231
00:16:01,900 --> 00:16:05,120
One of the earliest cultures to practice the science of dredging

232
00:16:05,121 --> 00:16:08,420
was that of the Phoenicians around the 13th century B.C.

233
00:16:09,380 --> 00:16:12,420
Early on, they were the leaders, you might say,

234
00:16:12,421 --> 00:16:14,800
in the maritime business,

235
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:16,680
and they were progressive

236
00:16:16,681 --> 00:16:19,020
in terms of their military activities.

237
00:16:19,260 --> 00:16:20,540
They had to have a way

238
00:16:20,541 --> 00:16:22,560
to get the craft

239
00:16:22,561 --> 00:16:25,560
into the shoreline areas

240
00:16:25,561 --> 00:16:28,240
to load their soldiers

241
00:16:28,241 --> 00:16:29,740
so they could move them

242
00:16:29,741 --> 00:16:31,780
to some other area.

243
00:16:32,380 --> 00:16:33,940
The Phoenician dredge

244
00:16:33,941 --> 00:16:35,600
consisted of a floating platform

245
00:16:35,601 --> 00:16:36,600
fitted with a tripod.

246
00:16:37,660 --> 00:16:38,640
On top of the tripod

247
00:16:38,641 --> 00:16:39,760
was a long pole

248
00:16:39,761 --> 00:16:41,400
equipped with a basket at one end.

249
00:16:42,420 --> 00:16:47,420
workers would lower the basket to scoop up loose sediment

250
00:16:47,421 --> 00:16:50,420
and bring it to the surface.

251
00:16:50,421 --> 00:16:57,420
As ancient cultures began to depend more and more on shipping for trade instead of war,

252
00:16:57,421 --> 00:17:00,420
sophisticated dredging machines emerged.

253
00:17:00,421 --> 00:17:05,420
By the 14th century, innovators introduced mechanical dredgers in Europe.

254
00:17:05,421 --> 00:17:11,420
Later, steel and the internal combustion engine enabled these earthmovers to achieve gargantuan dimensions

255
00:17:11,421 --> 00:17:13,420
and far greater efficiency.

256
00:17:17,440 --> 00:17:23,060
The Atlantic can rapidly fill up a scow that's able to haul 5,000 cubic yards of muck,

257
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:26,200
or 157 bucketfuls.

258
00:17:27,380 --> 00:17:31,880
If you're digging in mud with a large bucket, big mud bucket,

259
00:17:31,960 --> 00:17:35,380
you can load this scow probably in two or three hours.

260
00:17:35,740 --> 00:17:38,080
Scooping up soft mud is one thing.

261
00:17:38,081 --> 00:17:42,080
Digging up and lifting heavy rock is another.

262
00:17:42,081 --> 00:17:46,080
For this challenge, get behind the controls of this monster.

263
00:17:46,081 --> 00:17:50,080
A backhoe dredge.

264
00:17:50,081 --> 00:17:54,080
The steel teeth of a backhoe dredge bucket

265
00:17:54,081 --> 00:17:58,080
can rip through solid rock with up to 370,000 pounds of force.

266
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:06,080
The New York is the largest backhoe dredge in the world.

267
00:18:06,081 --> 00:18:11,080
In one load, it can bring up a staggering 26 tons of rock.

268
00:18:11,081 --> 00:18:15,080
But raw power isn't all this giant has going for it.

269
00:18:15,081 --> 00:18:18,080
It's got a brain to go with its brawn.

270
00:18:21,080 --> 00:18:24,080
The computer's telling me how much material I still got to pick up.

271
00:18:24,081 --> 00:18:28,080
You know, it tells me where to dig, my digging lines,

272
00:18:28,081 --> 00:18:33,080
the actual depth of all the material that I need to pick up.

273
00:18:33,081 --> 00:18:35,040
to pick up.

274
00:18:35,041 --> 00:18:38,840
While mechanical dredges like the New York scoop,

275
00:18:38,841 --> 00:18:41,140
hydraulic dredges suck.

276
00:18:41,141 --> 00:18:44,020
And we mean that in a good way.

277
00:18:44,021 --> 00:18:49,280
Hydraulic dredges operate like super-powered vacuum cleaners,

278
00:18:49,281 --> 00:18:53,680
utilizing powerful pumps to suction material to the surface.

279
00:18:53,681 --> 00:18:57,220
The pumps connect to long pipes that extend down

280
00:18:57,221 --> 00:19:00,340
to the floor of the channel.

281
00:19:00,341 --> 00:19:02,380
The end of the pipe can be fitted with a variety

282
00:19:02,381 --> 00:19:05,120
of different heads, depending on the job at hand.

283
00:19:06,860 --> 00:19:10,420
The most common type of hydraulic dredge used in the United States

284
00:19:10,421 --> 00:19:13,080
is known as a cutter suction dredge.

285
00:19:13,740 --> 00:19:18,180
It uses a bladed head to fracture rock or loosen hardened ground.

286
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:23,060
The bladed head revolves around an opening or mouth at the end of the pipe.

287
00:19:24,100 --> 00:19:29,220
As the blade penetrates the seabed, the fractured material is sucked through the mouth

288
00:19:29,221 --> 00:19:32,600
and emptied in a disposal or holding area on the surface.

289
00:19:34,160 --> 00:19:39,360
The Hydraulic Dredge Florida is one of the largest cutter suction dredges in the United States.

290
00:19:41,080 --> 00:19:43,920
It's got a 3,000 horsepower cutter head.

291
00:19:44,280 --> 00:19:47,600
The pumping power is about 12,000 horsepower between the two pumps that we have.

292
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:52,420
Another type of hydraulic behemoth is the hopper dredge.

293
00:19:52,680 --> 00:19:58,280
It's a self-powered vessel that can store huge amounts of material in a holding tank or hopper.

294
00:19:59,220 --> 00:20:02,120
It's often used to remove sandbars far off the coast.

295
00:20:04,860 --> 00:20:07,620
For quick removal of sediment in shallow waters,

296
00:20:07,720 --> 00:20:12,120
such as rivers, engineers rely on hydraulic dustpan dredges.

297
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:14,960
You'll find these along the muddy Mississippi.

298
00:20:17,620 --> 00:20:20,880
Dredges may have become more and more specialized and complex,

299
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:23,420
but their basic function remains simple.

300
00:20:23,421 --> 00:20:32,240
Still, they may become even more specialized in the coming years if the demand for overseas

301
00:20:32,241 --> 00:20:35,920
commerce continues to grow.

302
00:20:35,921 --> 00:20:40,660
As dredges reshape the ground beneath the surface of the water, other earth-moving wonders

303
00:20:40,661 --> 00:20:46,660
transform the ground beneath the surface of the earth.

304
00:20:46,661 --> 00:20:51,420
According to the American Association of Port Authorities, about 400 million cubic yards

305
00:20:51,421 --> 00:20:56,820
of material are dredged each year from American waters.

306
00:20:56,821 --> 00:21:00,500
That's enough to build a four-lane highway 20 feet deep,

307
00:21:00,501 --> 00:21:05,860
stretching from New York City to Los Angeles.

308
00:21:05,861 --> 00:21:10,140
Just outside Cleveland, Ohio, several hundred feet

309
00:21:10,141 --> 00:21:14,440
below the surface of the ground, a giant drill known

310
00:21:14,441 --> 00:21:20,820
as a tunnel boring machine cuts its way through ancient rock.

311
00:21:20,821 --> 00:21:23,620
It's been tunneling down here for over a year

312
00:21:23,621 --> 00:21:26,460
and has carved a lateral shaft stretching two and a half

313
00:21:26,461 --> 00:21:28,460
miles so far.

314
00:21:28,461 --> 00:21:33,040
This whole project is being built as a storage tunnel

315
00:21:33,041 --> 00:21:36,560
to store stormwater and sewage runoff.

316
00:21:36,561 --> 00:21:39,980
What happens is that during the first surge of a rainfall,

317
00:21:39,981 --> 00:21:43,280
the sewers overflow into this storage tunnel.

318
00:21:43,281 --> 00:21:46,040
And then at a later time, it's pumped out of this tunnel

319
00:21:46,041 --> 00:21:49,180
into the treatment plants.

320
00:21:49,181 --> 00:21:54,760
Two shifts of 10 crewmen operate this mammoth machine 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

321
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:59,660
For these workers, getting to the job site takes several unique modes of transportation.

322
00:22:03,560 --> 00:22:07,840
This particular project, we're going down underground now about 300 feet.

323
00:22:07,980 --> 00:22:09,940
We're in something called a man cage.

324
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:13,080
We're suspended from the work crane over here.

325
00:22:13,081 --> 00:22:16,760
And right now, beneath your feet, 300 feet straight down.

326
00:22:16,761 --> 00:22:21,760
The crane lowers workers through a vertical shaft to the tunnel floor.

327
00:22:21,761 --> 00:22:27,760
Next, they take a train traversing the two and a half mile cavity that their boring machine

328
00:22:27,761 --> 00:22:30,760
has already excavated.

329
00:22:30,761 --> 00:22:34,760
After 10 minutes, they reach the back end of this giant mechanical slug burrowing its

330
00:22:34,761 --> 00:22:37,760
way through the earth.

331
00:22:37,761 --> 00:22:42,760
The tunnel boring machine was built by the Robins Company at their manufacturing plant

332
00:22:42,761 --> 00:22:49,000
plant in Austintown, Ohio, and was lowered into the tunnel by a crane, one piece at a time.

333
00:22:49,960 --> 00:22:54,220
The tunnel boring machine was built in this first 200-foot section of tunnel,

334
00:22:54,720 --> 00:23:00,160
hence it had to be a little bit larger than what the tunnel boring machine wound up excavating.

335
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:05,800
The tunnel borer is equipped with two gripper arms on each side that stabilize the machine

336
00:23:05,801 --> 00:23:07,328
against the tunnel wall.

337
00:23:07,329 --> 00:23:11,720
As the cutting head grinds away, the grippers slowly push the machine

338
00:23:11,721 --> 00:23:12,421
forward.

339
00:23:12,422 --> 00:23:18,880
Buckets located inside the cutting head scoop up the fractured rock and dump it onto a

340
00:23:18,881 --> 00:23:19,950
conveyor belt.

341
00:23:19,951 --> 00:23:26,140
It's then whisked away the length of the tunnel and up a shaft where it's finally

342
00:23:26,141 --> 00:23:31,712
dumped on the surface. This sewage tunnel is still a work in progress.

343
00:23:31,713 --> 00:23:34,020
A half mile still needs to be

344
00:23:34,021 --> 00:23:34,721
mined.

345
00:23:34,722 --> 00:23:41,300
When the tunnel is fully excavated, the boring machine, or TBM, will be disassembled

346
00:23:41,301 --> 00:23:43,760
and removed the same way it was brought in.

347
00:23:47,200 --> 00:23:49,880
The rule that the shortest distance between two points

348
00:23:49,881 --> 00:23:52,480
is a straight line is one that has always

349
00:23:52,481 --> 00:23:54,980
propelled the tunneling industry.

350
00:23:54,981 --> 00:23:57,140
Instead of building a road around a mountain,

351
00:23:57,141 --> 00:23:58,020
you go through it.

352
00:24:00,520 --> 00:24:03,720
Archaeological studies reveal that our prehistoric ancestors

353
00:24:03,721 --> 00:24:06,900
were adept at designing crude tools from animal bones

354
00:24:06,901 --> 00:24:09,520
for a multitude of uses, including digging.

355
00:24:11,300 --> 00:24:15,460
They're nice and light. They're pointed. They're a wonderful scraping instrument.

356
00:24:16,480 --> 00:24:19,160
But you can only dig so far with the bone of an animal.

357
00:24:20,660 --> 00:24:28,340
By the 19th century, shovels, picks, drills, and explosives brought tunneling some sophistication.

358
00:24:30,300 --> 00:24:35,360
Although none of these tools provided the kind of precision that came later with tunnel boring machines.

359
00:24:35,361 --> 00:24:41,240
One of the main advantages is that you get a cut diameter fixed all the way through.

360
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:45,020
Your concrete cost, therefore, is almost unknown.

361
00:24:45,620 --> 00:24:49,160
Where if you drill and blast, you have a lot of overbreak.

362
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:51,480
Your cost of your concrete goes up considerable.

363
00:24:52,220 --> 00:24:58,520
That can amount to total savings on the job in the vicinity of 20 to 25 percent over drill and blast methods.

364
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:04,820
Tunnel boring machines have come in especially handy for creating tunnels in urban environments.

365
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:10,940
where the noise and traffic tie-ups caused by explosions can be unacceptable.

366
00:25:11,560 --> 00:25:13,760
And they're much better for building small bore tunnels,

367
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:16,440
which are too small for workers to gain access.

368
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:21,780
Herring Connect, a German-based manufacturer of tunnel boring machines,

369
00:25:22,860 --> 00:25:24,600
produces models in all sizes.

370
00:25:25,840 --> 00:25:29,800
Its largest sports a cutter head with a diameter spanning 47 feet.

371
00:25:29,801 --> 00:25:37,980
The factory does the complete fabrication, the complete machining, the assembly, it's

372
00:25:37,981 --> 00:25:43,860
assembled in the shop, it's fully tested, and then it's broken down into shipping, and

373
00:25:43,861 --> 00:25:46,560
then it's shipped to the particular job site.

374
00:25:46,561 --> 00:25:53,040
Each machine Heron Connect builds is custom made for a contractor working a specific job.

375
00:25:53,041 --> 00:25:58,180
When the job is finished, the contractor might disassemble and store the tunnel borer, sell

376
00:25:58,181 --> 00:26:00,882
it or reassemble it for another project.

377
00:26:00,883 --> 00:26:04,260
These sophisticated drilling machines might appear

378
00:26:04,261 --> 00:26:11,300
to be the pinnacle in tunneling technology, but Heron Connect, Robins and other TBM manufacturers

379
00:26:11,301 --> 00:26:15,940
have plans for even better machines.

380
00:26:15,941 --> 00:26:23,776
I think the future of tunnel boring machines in particular is in automation.

381
00:26:23,777 --> 00:26:25,220
One day you'll

382
00:26:25,221 --> 00:26:30,460
You'll see a tunnel boring machine, large diameter, excavating below a city with nobody

383
00:26:30,461 --> 00:26:33,460
actually physically in the tunnel.

384
00:26:33,461 --> 00:26:39,900
The excavation will be occurring remote control from an office above the tunnel.

385
00:26:39,901 --> 00:26:42,760
All the lining will be done automatically.

386
00:26:42,761 --> 00:26:47,140
All the equipment brought into the machine will be done automatically and everything

387
00:26:47,141 --> 00:26:50,620
can be controlled from the surface.

388
00:26:50,621 --> 00:26:54,900
When tunnel borers push through the earth and move it to the surface, it's a great achievement.

389
00:26:54,901 --> 00:27:01,900
When a plow turns the earth upside down, it can sometimes trigger a disaster.

390
00:27:01,901 --> 00:27:10,900
Some estimates place the amount of earth moved by humans each year at 45 billion tons.

391
00:27:10,901 --> 00:27:20,900
Giant trucks and dredges and tunnel borers move massive amounts of earth every day.

392
00:27:20,901 --> 00:27:24,360
Far more than an average farmer could hope to move in an entire year.

393
00:27:26,260 --> 00:27:30,940
Farmers move just a little bit of earth a few inches with a humble and unassuming plow.

394
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:38,640
Yet this simple revolutionary mechanism helped contribute to a series of catastrophes in the 1930s

395
00:27:38,641 --> 00:27:41,200
from which some farmers are still recovering.

396
00:27:42,100 --> 00:27:42,880
The Dust Bowl.

397
00:27:45,860 --> 00:27:50,320
97 million acres of lush farmlands turned into a desert.

398
00:27:50,900 --> 00:27:57,580
The wind blew away the earth and the topsoil and forced thousands from the prairie they

399
00:27:57,581 --> 00:28:01,120
called home.

400
00:28:01,121 --> 00:28:05,780
Some escaped just ahead of so-called black rollers that blotted out the sun.

401
00:28:05,781 --> 00:28:09,780
You'd run for cover and after a while you learn not to worry about the chickens and

402
00:28:09,781 --> 00:28:12,280
the cows, but to run for cover yourself.

403
00:28:12,281 --> 00:28:18,340
I interviewed people that said they would put in clotheslines or ropes between the barn

404
00:28:18,341 --> 00:28:21,040
and the house, or between the outhouse and the house,

405
00:28:21,041 --> 00:28:23,040
because you couldn't find yourself around.

406
00:28:23,041 --> 00:28:25,240
It was like being in a black blizzard.

407
00:28:28,220 --> 00:28:30,520
Throughout the history of agriculture,

408
00:28:30,521 --> 00:28:33,420
farmers have relied on various incarnations of the plow

409
00:28:33,421 --> 00:28:35,460
to break the land and turn the soil.

410
00:28:38,020 --> 00:28:41,160
In 1794, the first iron plow that was developed

411
00:28:41,161 --> 00:28:42,940
was by Thomas Jefferson.

412
00:28:42,941 --> 00:28:46,600
Approximately 25 years later, the first steel plow came out

413
00:28:46,601 --> 00:28:48,280
and then it had interchangeable parts.

414
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:51,440
With the new steel plow, we could go down deeper

415
00:28:51,441 --> 00:28:54,060
and move a tremendous amount of soil.

416
00:28:55,120 --> 00:28:56,820
By the mid-19th century,

417
00:28:57,260 --> 00:29:00,160
steel plows were revolutionizing agricultural production.

418
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:02,560
But in the decades to come,

419
00:29:03,480 --> 00:29:05,260
farmers learned that these efficient tools

420
00:29:05,261 --> 00:29:06,740
had a disturbing downside.

421
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:12,000
Steel plow that could break that really tough prairie sod.

422
00:29:12,420 --> 00:29:14,640
And of course, once you broke it, it didn't heal.

423
00:29:14,641 --> 00:29:18,560
it was scarred forever and the wind could get to it and the elements could get to it.

424
00:29:19,260 --> 00:29:24,700
Early in the 20th century, another technological breakthrough helped set the stage for the Dust Bowl.

425
00:29:25,740 --> 00:29:29,420
About 1915, tractors just started coming onto the horizon.

426
00:29:29,660 --> 00:29:31,600
Now agriculture started kicking into high gear.

427
00:29:32,180 --> 00:29:34,100
Tractors started really becoming on the forefront.

428
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:39,620
We were farming 40 acres now versus 3 acres when we were with horses.

429
00:29:39,621 --> 00:29:47,100
Yet another factor that led to the Dust Bowl was the enormous demand for wheat during World War I.

430
00:29:48,660 --> 00:29:54,600
Before the widespread use of motorized plows, just as the virgin prairie of southwestern Kansas was settled,

431
00:29:55,540 --> 00:29:58,300
farmers plowed under the grass and sowed the land with wheat.

432
00:30:00,860 --> 00:30:04,440
The earth was turned upside down by the farmer and his plow,

433
00:30:04,441 --> 00:30:08,480
multiplied by thousands of farmers with thousands of plows

434
00:30:08,481 --> 00:30:11,620
on millions of acres of once pristine prairie.

435
00:30:12,500 --> 00:30:14,860
They called themselves sodbusters,

436
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:17,620
and they bragged about breaking the soil

437
00:30:17,621 --> 00:30:19,840
the way you would break a horse or break an animal,

438
00:30:19,960 --> 00:30:21,400
and that's what they did. They broke it.

439
00:30:21,940 --> 00:30:24,140
By stripping the land of its natural vegetation,

440
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:28,640
the sodbusters had destroyed the delicate ecological balance of the plains,

441
00:30:30,280 --> 00:30:33,140
leaving nothing to hold the soil if the rain stopped

442
00:30:33,141 --> 00:30:34,560
and the wind blew.

443
00:30:35,620 --> 00:30:38,240
But throughout the 1920s, the weather was good.

444
00:30:38,840 --> 00:30:40,880
The demand and price of wheat remained high,

445
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:42,580
and farmers were prosperous.

446
00:30:44,680 --> 00:30:49,260
Then in October 1929, the stock market crashed,

447
00:30:49,780 --> 00:30:52,660
and farmers braced to reap what Wall Street had sown.

448
00:30:54,020 --> 00:30:55,420
The bottom fell out,

449
00:30:55,700 --> 00:30:58,840
and wheat went from $1 a bushel to 25 cents.

450
00:30:59,620 --> 00:31:02,300
It now cost more to grow wheat than to sell it.

451
00:31:03,140 --> 00:31:06,380
So farmers stopped growing and fields lay fallow.

452
00:31:10,680 --> 00:31:14,560
Season after season, the topsoil was plowed deep into the subsoil.

453
00:31:16,300 --> 00:31:21,220
When rains dwindled, the topsoil dried out and eventually turned to dust.

454
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:28,560
Fierce winds followed, blowing across the dry fields and stirring up great storms of dust.

455
00:31:28,561 --> 00:31:36,040
anyone who ever lived through it can tell you about it it's a really frightening thing of having

456
00:31:36,041 --> 00:31:43,300
darkness at midday they would wet bandanas or wet claws or towels and put them over their face like

457
00:31:43,301 --> 00:31:48,060
this to screen out the dust so you weren't breathing that stuff in people died of what

458
00:31:48,061 --> 00:31:54,220
they called the dust bowl pneumonia breathing in that dust the drought continued for months

459
00:31:54,221 --> 00:31:57,220
The months turned into years.

460
00:31:57,221 --> 00:31:59,220
Finally, families began leaving,

461
00:31:59,221 --> 00:32:03,220
abandoning their farms to seek hope elsewhere.

462
00:32:03,221 --> 00:32:07,220
But most stayed and suffered because they had nowhere to run.

463
00:32:07,221 --> 00:32:09,220
Dust would blow in through the keyhole,

464
00:32:09,221 --> 00:32:12,220
so you wanted to get those wet towels and rags down

465
00:32:12,221 --> 00:32:14,220
to dust-proof your house.

466
00:32:14,221 --> 00:32:15,220
And, of course, it was never possible.

467
00:32:15,221 --> 00:32:16,220
It would get in the chimney.

468
00:32:16,221 --> 00:32:18,220
It would get in through somewhere or other.

469
00:32:18,221 --> 00:32:20,220
And so whenever you went into your house,

470
00:32:20,221 --> 00:32:22,220
the damn stuff was all over you.

471
00:32:22,221 --> 00:32:24,220
You'd rub a table and your hand would be dirty.

472
00:32:24,840 --> 00:32:29,480
Some counties in southwestern Kansas lost nearly half their population.

473
00:32:30,860 --> 00:32:34,360
Abandoned, uncultivated fields only fed the dusty winds.

474
00:32:35,120 --> 00:32:39,380
Earth that took thousands of years for nature to craft blew away in minutes.

475
00:32:40,360 --> 00:32:43,120
To have dust everywhere and know that you could never win that fight,

476
00:32:43,200 --> 00:32:45,640
that there was going to be dust all through your body,

477
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:49,940
all through your clothing, through your food, that you couldn't escape it,

478
00:32:49,941 --> 00:32:51,320
it must have been a nightmare.

479
00:32:52,220 --> 00:32:57,740
By 1934, over 650,000 tons of topsoil had blown away.

480
00:32:58,920 --> 00:33:04,120
After 10 years of unrelenting drought, dust and depression, it ended.

481
00:33:04,720 --> 00:33:08,000
The rains returned, making the soil less likely to blow.

482
00:33:08,900 --> 00:33:10,920
Grasses grew back in the dormant fields.

483
00:33:12,140 --> 00:33:15,360
Another world war soon raised the demand and the price for wheat.

484
00:33:15,900 --> 00:33:18,020
Prosperity had returned to the Great Plains,

485
00:33:18,021 --> 00:33:21,540
but farmers were determined to figure out why the Dust Bowl had occurred.

486
00:33:22,220 --> 00:33:25,000
and what they could do to keep it from happening again.

487
00:33:26,020 --> 00:33:28,043
Beginning in the 1940s, the U.S.

488
00:33:28,044 --> 00:33:31,520
Department of Agriculture's Wind Erosion Research Unit,

489
00:33:31,940 --> 00:33:35,100
located in Manhattan, Kansas, has been doing just that.

490
00:33:35,680 --> 00:33:38,400
I think our unit exists because of the dust bowls.

491
00:33:39,480 --> 00:33:43,620
The dust bowl was instrumental in creating the Soil Conservation Service.

492
00:33:44,580 --> 00:33:48,680
It was dust settling in Washington from the Great Plains.

493
00:33:48,681 --> 00:33:52,780
The unit studied the causes of drought and the mechanics of dust storms.

494
00:33:53,600 --> 00:33:57,840
So our mission is to understand the processes of wind erosion,

495
00:33:58,640 --> 00:34:02,680
develop predictive tools and control practices,

496
00:34:03,040 --> 00:34:07,140
and then transfer this technology to the people who need and use it.

497
00:34:07,460 --> 00:34:11,820
The Wind Erosion Unit advised farmers on soil conservation techniques,

498
00:34:12,100 --> 00:34:15,080
from how to prepare the earth to how to till their fields.

499
00:34:15,081 --> 00:34:24,740
We learned that we couldn't rely on just one system to take care of all of our crop production.

500
00:34:24,960 --> 00:34:26,200
We had to rotate crops.

501
00:34:26,600 --> 00:34:30,260
We knew that we had to go in in certain areas that were prone to high erosion rates

502
00:34:30,261 --> 00:34:33,840
to go in and cover these areas with something that would prevent erosion.

503
00:34:34,720 --> 00:34:39,260
As the government did its part to prevent another dust bowl, so did the farmers.

504
00:34:40,040 --> 00:34:42,520
They terraced their fields in order to conserve water.

505
00:34:43,080 --> 00:34:45,760
They developed irrigation systems to stave off drought.

506
00:34:47,660 --> 00:34:50,620
They planted trees as barriers against the wind.

507
00:34:51,460 --> 00:34:54,420
And they didn't use their tractors as intensely as before.

508
00:34:55,740 --> 00:34:58,480
The 1950s saw the return of drought.

509
00:34:59,080 --> 00:35:00,760
The skies again filled with dust.

510
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:03,680
But this time, the farmers were better prepared.

511
00:35:04,240 --> 00:35:07,860
New farming techniques helped them weather the crisis and those that followed.

512
00:35:09,240 --> 00:35:11,400
We've had numerous droughts afterwards.

513
00:35:11,401 --> 00:35:14,020
We just had a pretty bad one here in 1988.

514
00:35:14,620 --> 00:35:16,000
But the farmers have gotten better.

515
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:17,460
The technology is better.

516
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:22,340
So the farmers can go out there and farm the ground without causing an erosion problem.

517
00:35:23,980 --> 00:35:29,660
The plow and the farmers who used it weren't the only factors that contributed to the dust bowl of the 1930s.

518
00:35:31,220 --> 00:35:36,200
Today we have a better understanding of the natural and economic cycles that also played a role.

519
00:35:37,640 --> 00:35:39,580
Farmers no longer bust the sod.

520
00:35:39,581 --> 00:35:44,280
They work it, they nurture it, and they support it, just as it helps support them.

521
00:35:45,140 --> 00:35:52,840
And when they do plow the land, they do so with the knowledge and experience learned from those dusty, windswept, and desolate days.

522
00:35:56,360 --> 00:36:00,120
According to recent statistics from the United States Department of Agriculture,

523
00:36:00,960 --> 00:36:08,080
approximately 800 million tons of non-federal cropland soil is destroyed by wind each year.

524
00:36:09,580 --> 00:36:14,580
Humans are moving dirt all over the planet at an unprecedented rate.

525
00:36:14,581 --> 00:36:19,580
Technology now allows us literally to alter the face of the earth.

526
00:36:19,581 --> 00:36:24,580
In the last century we're seeing a huge change in our ability to move soil around.

527
00:36:24,581 --> 00:36:29,580
A farmer a hundred years ago would take a whole day to do three acres.

528
00:36:29,581 --> 00:36:32,580
Now we can do an acre in three minutes.

529
00:36:32,581 --> 00:36:38,580
Our prodigious earth moving efforts are destroying earth faster than it can be replaced.

530
00:36:38,581 --> 00:36:47,020
Nature crafts soil every day, created by seismic and environmental factors such as wind, gravity, rain, and decay.

531
00:36:48,160 --> 00:36:50,480
And it's destroyed by the same factors.

532
00:36:51,440 --> 00:36:55,960
Rivers wash soil into the oceans, and wind blows dust into the atmosphere.

533
00:36:57,560 --> 00:36:59,900
In fact, we may actually run out of Earth.

534
00:37:00,620 --> 00:37:04,840
As humans put greater burdens on the soil to support our ever-increasing population,

535
00:37:05,760 --> 00:37:10,760
We may have no place else to turn but up to the fourth planet from the sun.

536
00:37:12,980 --> 00:37:16,240
This is Mars, but it could very well be a lot of places on Earth.

537
00:37:16,760 --> 00:37:19,920
If you were standing out on this desert the way I appear to be right now,

538
00:37:20,180 --> 00:37:21,400
it would not be very healthy.

539
00:37:22,020 --> 00:37:23,560
The atmosphere is extremely thin,

540
00:37:23,561 --> 00:37:25,960
100 times thinner than the atmosphere here on Earth.

541
00:37:27,280 --> 00:37:29,800
So it would be an instantly lethal environment.

542
00:37:30,800 --> 00:37:33,540
The Martian air isn't only lethally thin,

543
00:37:33,541 --> 00:37:36,200
It's also extremely cold.

544
00:37:36,201 --> 00:37:40,880
The mean temperature is minus 81 degrees Fahrenheit.

545
00:37:40,881 --> 00:37:44,100
But Earth movers, or in this case, Mars movers,

546
00:37:44,101 --> 00:37:47,100
may someday be able to thicken and warm the Martian atmosphere

547
00:37:47,101 --> 00:37:48,600
enough to make it habitable.

548
00:37:53,180 --> 00:37:55,980
Trapped in the Martian soil may be compounds that

549
00:37:55,981 --> 00:37:58,780
can be turned into greenhouse gases.

550
00:37:58,781 --> 00:38:02,100
If these warm gases were created and released,

551
00:38:02,101 --> 00:38:04,300
they would melt the planet's polar ice caps

552
00:38:04,301 --> 00:38:06,380
and transform the Martian atmosphere

553
00:38:06,381 --> 00:38:08,540
into one resembling our own.

554
00:38:08,980 --> 00:38:11,000
On Earth, the industrial processes

555
00:38:11,001 --> 00:38:13,540
that make these greenhouse gases are quite sophisticated.

556
00:38:14,280 --> 00:38:16,440
On Mars, we're going to have to literally start with dirt

557
00:38:16,441 --> 00:38:17,600
and start with Mars air

558
00:38:17,601 --> 00:38:19,600
and figure out some process to do that.

559
00:38:20,460 --> 00:38:23,580
What we would imagine is sending small units,

560
00:38:23,720 --> 00:38:24,940
small factories to Mars

561
00:38:24,941 --> 00:38:27,340
that would scoop up some dirt, suck in Mars air,

562
00:38:28,000 --> 00:38:29,560
undergo some chemical transformation,

563
00:38:29,780 --> 00:38:31,840
and release these greenhouse gases.

564
00:38:32,100 --> 00:38:35,740
and they just go around like ants around the Martian surface.

565
00:38:35,860 --> 00:38:37,500
And you might imagine thousands of these,

566
00:38:37,920 --> 00:38:40,040
all autonomously moving around Mars,

567
00:38:40,460 --> 00:38:43,920
processing the material, making these greenhouse gases.

568
00:38:44,140 --> 00:38:46,100
And we've done calculations that suggest

569
00:38:46,101 --> 00:38:48,760
if we could produce these greenhouse gases

570
00:38:48,761 --> 00:38:51,160
at a concentration of only a part per million

571
00:38:51,161 --> 00:38:52,560
in the Martian atmosphere,

572
00:38:52,561 --> 00:38:55,860
it would warm up the planet enough to start the process

573
00:38:55,861 --> 00:38:59,540
of melting the polar caps and releasing the CO2 in the water.

574
00:38:59,541 --> 00:39:04,540
What would follow is an extraordinary process known as terraforming, or ecosynthesis.

575
00:39:05,540 --> 00:39:08,540
If we imagine Mars now becoming slightly warmer,

576
00:39:08,541 --> 00:39:11,540
now it's starting to look like the coldest places on Earth.

577
00:39:11,541 --> 00:39:15,540
As it warms up a little more, it might become like the top of a mountain.

578
00:39:16,540 --> 00:39:21,540
Sparse, tundra-like life, and just small bushes, small plants.

579
00:39:21,541 --> 00:39:24,540
And as it warms more, you can imagine coming down the mountain.

580
00:39:24,541 --> 00:39:25,241
the mountain.

581
00:39:25,242 --> 00:39:28,980
And eventually, the key point in the history of Mars will be when it gets

582
00:39:28,981 --> 00:39:31,220
warm enough that the first trees can grow.

583
00:39:32,020 --> 00:39:35,518
So how long would it take for this epic transformation to unfold?

584
00:39:35,519 --> 00:39:36,540
You'd be surprised.

585
00:39:38,420 --> 00:39:42,540
We're not talking about something that's going to take millions of years or that has to wait

586
00:39:42,541 --> 00:39:44,766
for Star Trek inventions to happen.

587
00:39:44,767 --> 00:39:48,900
We're talking about something that could result in a biologically

588
00:39:48,901 --> 00:39:50,480
rich world in 100 years.

589
00:39:50,481 --> 00:39:55,480
Maybe someday a Martian farmer will glance up over his shoulder at the big blue planet

590
00:39:55,481 --> 00:40:01,480
and be thankful that we learned so much about Earth moving back when we lived only on the Earth.

591
00:40:03,480 --> 00:40:07,480
That's assuming Earth isn't blown apart by a collision with an asteroid.

592
00:40:07,481 --> 00:40:12,480
But scientists are already speculating about ways to prevent such a calamity,

593
00:40:12,481 --> 00:40:18,480
using a new breed of Earth movers, powerful enough to deflect any heavenly body racing our way.

594
00:40:18,481 --> 00:40:22,480
We think there's anywhere from 1,000 to 2,000 objects

595
00:40:22,520 --> 00:40:25,320
about a mile in diameter or larger

596
00:40:25,360 --> 00:40:27,720
that are within the Earth's neighborhood.

597
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:29,720
But it's a big neighborhood.

598
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:32,120
Scientists define near-Earth objects

599
00:40:32,160 --> 00:40:34,720
as those that get within roughly 30 million miles

600
00:40:34,760 --> 00:40:37,640
of Earth's orbit.

601
00:40:37,660 --> 00:40:41,640
Nevertheless, any body a mile across striking the Earth

602
00:40:41,680 --> 00:40:43,640
would be cataclysmic.

603
00:40:43,680 --> 00:40:46,840
We know of one concrete example

604
00:40:46,841 --> 00:40:50,920
of a terribly destructive impact 65 million years ago.

605
00:40:50,940 --> 00:40:53,440
An asteroid or comet hit in the Yucatan

606
00:40:53,480 --> 00:40:55,640
and killed most species on Earth,

607
00:40:55,680 --> 00:40:58,760
including most famously the dinosaurs.

608
00:40:58,780 --> 00:41:00,080
But don't worry.

609
00:41:00,120 --> 00:41:02,720
The number of large near-Earth objects is small,

610
00:41:02,760 --> 00:41:08,600
and scientists expect to hit only every 100,000 to 200,000 years.

611
00:41:08,620 --> 00:41:11,360
So the object is to try and find the vast majority

612
00:41:11,400 --> 00:41:14,360
of these objects and keep an eye on them.

613
00:41:14,361 --> 00:41:17,140
If we do spy one, headed in our direction,

614
00:41:17,780 --> 00:41:20,020
Earth movers will likely be ready to save the day.

615
00:41:21,960 --> 00:41:24,520
Scientists theorize that the most effective of these

616
00:41:24,521 --> 00:41:27,180
might be the most powerful Earth mover ever created,

617
00:41:27,760 --> 00:41:29,120
a hydrogen bomb.

618
00:41:30,220 --> 00:41:33,520
The most direct and probably the most efficient way of doing it

619
00:41:33,521 --> 00:41:36,160
would be to set off a nuclear weapon, not to blow it up,

620
00:41:36,520 --> 00:41:39,920
but simply to set it off near the surface of the asteroid

621
00:41:39,921 --> 00:41:42,680
so the neutron flux from this neutron bomb

622
00:41:42,681 --> 00:41:49,180
would ablate the front side of the asteroid and form a rocket-like thrusting of the material off the surface.

623
00:41:49,700 --> 00:41:54,360
And if we can give the asteroid just a tiny thrust, maybe a centimeter per second or so,

624
00:41:55,140 --> 00:42:00,420
over the 10 or 20 years that it would take for this asteroid to impact Earth,

625
00:42:01,020 --> 00:42:03,780
we would have moved it enough so that it would miss Earth.

626
00:42:05,880 --> 00:42:10,060
Other alternatives include sending a spacecraft to hover over the asteroid,

627
00:42:10,061 --> 00:42:13,400
where the thrust from its engine would create a gravity tractor

628
00:42:13,401 --> 00:42:15,420
that would pull the asteroid away.

629
00:42:17,060 --> 00:42:20,680
Or the spacecraft could attach itself to the asteroid to push it away.

630
00:42:23,500 --> 00:42:28,980
In February 2001, NASA took a baby step toward realizing such a scenario.

631
00:42:30,380 --> 00:42:33,140
NASA had a very successful asteroid mission

632
00:42:33,141 --> 00:42:36,380
to one of the largest near-Earth asteroids, Eros,

633
00:42:36,381 --> 00:42:40,400
in which the spacecraft orbited around the asteroid for a year

634
00:42:40,401 --> 00:42:44,180
and then landed and actually carried out measurements on the surface.

635
00:42:44,540 --> 00:42:47,700
So we certainly, with a scientific spacecraft, can do that,

636
00:42:48,020 --> 00:42:49,940
at least if we get to pick the asteroid.

637
00:42:50,540 --> 00:42:54,160
It's a little harder to do if it's the asteroid that picks us.

638
00:42:54,980 --> 00:42:57,120
Manipulating the orbit of a near-Earth object

639
00:42:57,121 --> 00:43:00,420
is one of the challenges scientists feel confident they can overcome

640
00:43:00,421 --> 00:43:03,100
before any object comes plummeting close to Earth.

641
00:43:03,101 --> 00:43:10,600
In fact, we may launch Earth movers to mine asteroids and comets for minerals and fuels

642
00:43:10,601 --> 00:43:12,860
as we begin to colonize the solar system.

643
00:43:12,861 --> 00:43:19,600
The things that we really need in space, first of all water, it's iron, iron-nickel

644
00:43:19,601 --> 00:43:26,940
for steel, carbon, these things are present now in comets and asteroids.

645
00:43:26,941 --> 00:43:31,620
All sorts of new Earth movers are on the drawing board, both for our own planet and

646
00:43:31,621 --> 00:43:34,560
for those yet to be explored.

647
00:43:34,561 --> 00:43:37,060
What began centuries ago as a simple effort

648
00:43:37,061 --> 00:43:39,740
to keep us sheltered and fed has turned

649
00:43:39,741 --> 00:43:41,720
into an engineering legacy that's

650
00:43:41,721 --> 00:43:45,020
a hallmark of the modern age.

651
00:43:45,021 --> 00:43:47,140
Humans are the ultimate Earth movers,

652
00:43:47,141 --> 00:43:50,520
designing bigger trucks and scoops and drills,

653
00:43:50,521 --> 00:43:53,420
moving Earth and moving beyond Earth

654
00:43:53,421 --> 00:43:57,420
into a frontier limited only by the scope of our imagination.

